# Grammar

The Language Level 0.1–0.5 grammar remains described in the implementation brief
and the parser sources. Language Level 0.6 adds the comprehension forms below.
Comprehension targets remain a single `NAME`. Assignment and `for` statements
accept a fixed name list (D-0038). `star_expr`, async, nested unpack, and
generator expressions are not part of this increment.

Comparison operators include membership:

```ebnf
comp_op          ::= "<" | ">" | "==" | ">=" | "<=" | "!="
                   | "in" | "not" "in" | "is" | "is" "not"
```

```ebnf
expression       ::= or_test ["if" or_test "else" expression]
or_test          ::= and_test ("or" and_test)*
assignment       ::= target_list "=" expression_list
target_list      ::= NAME ("," NAME)* [","]
                   | "(" target_list ")"
expression_list  ::= expression ("," expression)* [","]
for_statement    ::= "for" target_list "in" expression ":" suite
                     [ "else" ":" suite ]
```

Conditional expressions bind less tightly than `or`. Else-chains are
right-associative (`a if c1 else b if c2 else c`). `lambda` is not part of
this grammar, so `expression` is the top of the Pratt/`or` ladder (D-0040).

Unparenthesized tuples remain invalid in other expression positions
(`return a, b` is still a syntax error; D-0015).

```ebnf
list_display     ::= "[" [starred_list | comprehension] "]"
set_display      ::= "{" starred_list | comprehension "}"
dict_display     ::= "{" "}" | "{" (key_datum_list | dict_comprehension) "}"

comprehension    ::= expression comp_for
dict_comprehension ::= expression ":" expression comp_for

comp_for         ::= "for" NAME "in" or_test [comp_iter]
comp_iter        ::= comp_for | comp_if
comp_if          ::= "if" or_test [comp_iter]
```

`{x for x in it}` is a set comprehension; `{k: v for k in it}` is a dict
comprehension; `{}` remains an empty dict. `(x for x in it)` is rejected with a
targeted diagnostic. Comprehension names bind in the enclosing scope (D-0026).
String iterables yield one-character strings.